  <h2>Using HRV Without Becoming Obsessed</h2>
  <p>
    The researchers who first described orthosomnia — the anxiety-driven over-engagement with sleep metrics — were responding to a real clinical pattern they observed in patients who had adopted consumer sleep trackers. The phenomenon is not inevitable, but it is worth designing around deliberately.
  </p>
  <p>
    The YumePad approach to HRV presentation is built around three principles that reduce the risk of orthosomnia:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Trend over snapshot.</strong> The primary HRV display shows a rolling 7-day and 30-day average, not last night's number in isolation. A single low-HRV night is contextualized within the distribution before the user sees it, which prevents the "disaster framing" that a raw score can produce.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Explanation over verdict.</strong> When HRV is low, the morning report tries to identify the probable cause from the behavioral context log — "Your HRV was below your 7-day average. Your context log shows alcohol at dinner and a later-than-usual bedtime. Both are consistent with the pattern." This explanatory framing converts a potentially anxiety-provoking number into a comprehensible data point with an identified cause — which is far less distressing than an unexplained bad score.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Permission to not look.</strong> The YumePad app allows users to set a "quiet mode" for the HRV display that shows only weekly summaries rather than nightly values. For users who find themselves checking their HRV first thing in the morning and letting it affect their day's mood and energy expectations, quiet mode preserves the longitudinal data benefit while removing the daily score-checking ritual that drives orthosomnia.
  </p>

  <h2>When HRV Signals Something Worth Investigating</h2>
  <p>
    The following HRV patterns in longitudinal data are worth discussing with a healthcare provider. They do not diagnose anything — but they represent anomalies that have clinical correlates in the research literature.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sustained HRV decline over 3+ weeks</strong> without an obvious behavioral explanation (illness, major stress, significant lifestyle change) can be an early indicator of developing cardiovascular dysfunction, autonomic neuropathy, or a sleep disorder such as undiagnosed OSA</li>
    <li><strong>Very low HRV during N3 with normal HRV during light sleep</strong> suggests that slow-wave sleep is nominally occurring but is being physiologically compromised — a pattern seen in mild sleep apnea, periodic limb movements, and certain medications</li>
    <li><strong>HRV that doesn't recover within a week after illness</strong> may indicate that the illness has had a more lasting autonomic effect than expected — this pattern has been observed in post-viral fatigue syndromes and warrants clinical follow-up</li>
    <li><strong>Sudden step-change decline in baseline HRV</strong> not explained by an identifiable acute event is statistically unusual and worth a clinical conversation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    SleepTech's commitment, rooted in the company's founding in Osaka in 2012 and its long-standing relationships with academic sleep medicine institutions across Japan, is to surface these patterns to users in a way that is informative without being alarmist — and to provide clear pathways to clinical evaluation when the data suggests one is warranted.
  </p>

